{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כל דבר רע כי תועבת", :translit "kol davar ra ki to'evat", :gloss "any evil thing for it is an abomination"} {:he "לוסר לך", :translit "l'aser lakh,", :gloss "to restrain you,"} {:he "כל המנבל את פיו נקרא תועבה ושנאוי", :translit "kol ha-m'nabel et piv nikra to'evah u's'nu'i", :gloss "everyone who pollutes his mouth is called an abomination and hated"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "reads the phrase as a warning against foul speech"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'for it is an abomination' (כי תועבת) serves in rabbinic tradition as a general prohibition that encompasses improper speech, as the term 'abomination' (תועבה) is applied to one who speaks obscenely (Shabbat 33a). The language of the verse is taken as addressed to the individual: 'to restrain yourself' from such behavior.", :sources ["Shabbat 33a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כל דבר רע", :translit "kol davar ra", :gloss "any evil thing"} {:he "איש או אשה אשר יעשה את הרע וגו'", :translit "ish o ishah asher ya'aseh et ha-ra v'gomer", :gloss "a man or a woman who does the evil…"} {:he "וילך ויעבוד אלהים אחרים", :translit "v'yeilekh v'ya'avod elohim aherim", :gloss "and goes and serves other elohim"} {:he "שהדיבור והמחשבה בעכו\"ם מצטרפים למעשה", :translit "she-ha-dibbur v'ha-machshavah ba-akum mitztarfim la-ma'aseh", :gloss "that speech and thought concerning idolatry combine with the deed"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "geyra (juxtaposition) of two verses sharing the root רע to derive that speech and thought of idolatry join the act"}], :frame {:text "The verse beginning 'a man or a woman who does the evil… and goes and serves other elohim' (Deuteronomy 17:2–3) refers to idolatry. The juxtaposition of 'any evil thing' to this passage teaches that speech and thought regarding idolatrous worship combine with the act to constitute liability, a principle rooted in the rabbinic analysis of the verse 'lest you inquire… and say, How do these nations serve their elohim, so I will do likewise' (Deuteronomy 12:30), which treats the inquiry itself as a transgression (Sanhedrin 61b).", :sources ["Sanhedrin 61b" "Deuteronomy 17:2–3" "Deuteronomy 12:30"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "reads the phrase as a warning against foul speech", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "geyra (juxtaposition) of two verses sharing the root רע to derive that speech and thought of idolatry join the act", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:07.639194025Z", :model "glm-5.2"}